<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Spanish Armada</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1588">1588</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Armada · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-spanish-war-1585-1604">Anglo-Spanish War (1585–1604)</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Spanish Armada was the fleet sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> in 1588 to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. It was defeated by the English navy and storms around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-isles">British Isles</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> treat the failure as a symbol of the limits of Spanish power, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> remained a great power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/spanish-armada.jpg" alt="Spanish Armada" width="320" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Robert Adams, Augustine Ryther · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Armadaroute.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> had been allies for many decades after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-vii-of-england">Henry VII of England</a> formed a strategic relationship with Ferdinand II of Spain against the threat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-wars-of-religion">French Wars of Religion</a> were the ultimate cause of the break between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a>. The fleet's purpose was to reinstate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> in England, end English support for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> and stop attacks by English and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> privateers on Spanish interests in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The fleet was commanded by Alonso de Guzmán, Duke of Medina Sidonia, an aristocrat appointed by Philip. When it left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> it numbered 141 ships, with 10,138 sailors and 19,315 soldiers. Its orders were to sail up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a>, join the army of Alexander Farnese, Duke of Parma, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> and escort an invasion force to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. The English fleet, based at Plymouth, was faster and more manoeuvrable than the larger Spanish galleons, and it kept beyond grappling range and bombarded the Spanish from a distance.</p><p>The armada anchored off Calais on 7 August. Parma's army, reduced by disease to 16,000, was not ready, and the barges meant to carry it would have had to cross waters dominated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> navy. That night English fire ships forced the Spanish to scatter. The armada suffered further losses at the Battle of Gravelines and was in danger of running aground on the Dutch coast when the wind changed and let it escape into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>. Pursued by the English, the ships returned by way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and Ireland, and up to 24 were wrecked on the way.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> the news brought shock, and the nation went into mourning. Philip is claimed to have said that he sent the armada against men, not God's winds and waves. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> the victory was marked with pamphlets, medals and celebrations, and it fed national pride for years. A medal bore the inscription meaning God blew, and they are scattered. England's own counter-armada of 1589 failed, and Spain sent three further armadas in 1596, 1597 and 1601, which also failed.</p><p>The English use of naval guns from windward, rather than ramming and boarding, has been seen as a revolution in naval tactics. Historians have long debated the causes. Early writers stressed God's favour, and later scholars have examined the comparative power of English and Spanish guns, the part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-drake">Francis Drake</a> and Charles Howard, and the weather.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Spanish Armada” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Spanish Armada</b> of 1588, sent against England, was defeated by the English fleet and storms.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Henry VIII built up a permanent navy, and in 1588 English ships helped defeat the <b>Spanish Armada</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…sea power was shown at Lepanto (1571), where Spanish galleys formed a major part of the Holy League fleet that defeated the Ottomans, but the <b>Armada</b> sent against England in 1588 was defeated by English ships and wrecked by storms on the voyage home round Scotland and Ireland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Off the coast of Cornwall, Admiral Miguel de Horna's <b>Armada</b> of Flanders intercepted an Anglo-Dutch merchant convoy of 44 vessels escorted by six Dutch warships, sinking or capturing 20 ships, and returned safely to its base at Dunkirk.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-18">18 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After nineteen years in English captivity, Mary was beheaded for plotting against Elizabeth I. Her execution removed the Catholic claimant to the English throne and helped provoke the <b>Spanish Armada</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-8">8 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Her 44-year reign saw a Protestant settlement, the <b>Armada</b>'s defeat and a flowering of literature.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-15">15 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
